<Layout title="Common Mistakes to Avoid When Selling an Inherited Property in Texas" description="Selling an inherited home in Texas? Here are the most common mistakes families make, from unclear authority to rushing the first offer, and how to avoid them.">

  <section class="page-hero navy-bg">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/blog" class="back-link">&larr; Back to Blog</a>
      <div class="post-meta" style="margin-top: 16px;">
        <span class="post-category">Inherited Property</span>
        <span class="post-date">November 2026</span>
      </div>
      <h1>Common Mistakes to Avoid When Selling an Inherited Property in Texas</h1>
      <p class="hero-sub">Most of the costly mistakes we see are avoidable. Here's what to watch for before you list, accept an offer, or clear out the house.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container article-layout">
      <article class="article-body">

        <div class="article-intro">
          <p>Selling an inherited home is different from selling a home you've lived in. There's often a legal process running in the background, more than one person involved in the decision, and a lot of emotion attached to the property. Under that kind of pressure, it's easy to make a move that costs time or money later. The good news is that nearly all of these mistakes are preventable once you know what to look for.</p>
          <p>We're licensed Texas Realtors, not attorneys or tax advisors. This guide covers the real estate side. For legal or tax questions specific to your situation, consult a probate attorney or CPA.</p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="authority">The Mistakes Worth Avoiding</h2>

        <div class="process-steps">
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Mistake 1</div>
            <h4>Listing before you know who has authority to sell</h4>
            <p>Not everyone who inherits a home can sign a deed on day one. Depending on the situation, authority may sit with an executor, an administrator, or all of the beneficiaries together. Starting a listing or accepting an offer before this is settled can stall a closing or put a deal at risk. Confirm who can legally sell with your probate attorney first.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Mistake 2</div>
            <h4>Skipping a date-of-death valuation</h4>
            <p>The value of the home on the date of the owner's death generally sets your tax basis. Without a documented value from around that time, it's harder to prove later. A formal appraisal or a comparative market analysis is inexpensive protection. We cover why this matters in our guide on <a href="/blog/how-capital-gains-tax-works-on-inherited-property-in-texas">capital gains tax on inherited property</a>.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Mistake 3</div>
            <h4>Clearing out the house too quickly</h4>
            <p>It's tempting to start emptying the home right away, especially if you live out of state. But the will may leave specific items to specific people, and some belongings carry real value or sentimental weight for other family members. Check with the estate's attorney before donating, discarding, or selling anything, and take photos of what's there.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Mistake 4</div>
            <h4>Letting the property sit vacant</h4>
            <p>An empty house still has bills, and it carries added risk. Many standard insurance policies limit coverage when a home is vacant for an extended period, and empty properties are more vulnerable to damage and break-ins. Keep insurance current, keep basic utilities on, and have someone check on the home regularly.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Mistake 5</div>
            <h4>Taking the first offer without comparing options</h4>
            <p>Unsolicited offers on inherited homes arrive quickly, and they're often priced low. A fast cash sale can be the right call, and so can a full market listing. The mistake is choosing either one without seeing the other in real numbers. Compare what each path nets you after costs and time before you decide.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Mistake 6</div>
            <h4>Not getting the other heirs on the same page</h4>
            <p>Disagreements between family members cause more delays than almost anything else. Talk early about whether to sell, what price is reasonable, and who handles what. Showing everyone the same valuation and the same options often resolves more than any argument will.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Mistake 7</div>
            <h4>Assuming a mortgage, liens, or back taxes block a sale</h4>
            <p>They usually don't. In most cases these are paid off from the sale proceeds at closing. The mistake is not finding out the payoff amounts early, because those numbers decide how much, if anything, the estate nets. Ask the title company for a payoff and lien search up front.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Mistake 8</div>
            <h4>Spending on repairs before running the numbers</h4>
            <p>Fresh paint and a new roof don't always pay for themselves, especially on a tight timeline. Before spending estate money, get an honest read on what repairs would add to the sale price compared with selling as-is. Sometimes the best return is no repairs at all.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="process-step">
            <div class="step-label">Mistake 9</div>
            <h4>Going it alone without the right professionals</h4>
            <p>A probate attorney, a CPA, and a real estate agent who understands inherited property each cover a different piece. The cost of getting advice early is almost always smaller than the cost of fixing a mistake after closing.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout-box">
          <strong>The common thread:</strong> Most of these mistakes come from moving before you have the facts, or from not moving at all. A little information up front, such as who can sell, what the home is worth, and what each path nets, makes the rest of the process much easier.
        </div>

        <h2 id="how-we-help">How We Can Help</h2>
        <p>We work with sellers of inherited property every week. We can give you an honest valuation, show you what a cash sale and a market listing would each look like, and coordinate with your attorney and the title company so the paperwork is ready when you are. If the best answer is something other than working with us, we'll tell you that.</p>

